Sounds reasonable.

The "%USERPROFILE%\Appdata" folder seems to be for "files generated by=20
applications" as SviMik said:

<https://technet.microsoft.com/en-us/library/cc962614.aspx>
<https://technet.microsoft.com/en-us/library/cc749104%28v=3Dws.10%29.aspx>

Those links refer to Windows XP's equivalent for $HOME\Appdata, but the=20
purpose of the directory has not changed afaik.

Things are complicated a bit more by the fact that the %APPDATA%=20
environment variable points to %USERPROFILE%\Appdata\Roaming, not
%USERPROFILE%\Appdata as one might think.

In the first link above, MS says this:

"CSIDL_PROFILE: The user's profile folder. A typical path is=20
C:\Documents and Settings\username. Applications should not create files=20
or folders at this level; they should put their data under the locations=20
referred to by CSIDL_APPDATA or CSIDL_LOCAL_APPDATA."

I think the idea Microsoft thought that users should not (have to) touch=20
the Appdata folder, and thus decided to hide it.

I vote for using a directory that is visible by default, at least until=20
the GUI can reliably import any config thrown at it.

